# What must never reach the API image's build context.
#
# The reason this file has to exist, rather than being a tidiness measure: the
# Dockerfile does `COPY src/ src/`, which without these rules copies the host's
# bin/ and obj/ folders in too. obj/ holds project.assets.json, and that file
# records absolute paths into the host's NuGet cache — on this machine,
# C:\Users\...\.nuget\packages. Inside a Linux container that path does not
# exist, so NuGet's FallbackPackagePathResolver throws before a single file is
# compiled and the build dies in `dotnet publish` with an MSB4018 stack trace
# that says nothing about the real cause.
#
# It also only fails on a machine that has built locally first, which is why a
# clean CI checkout passes and a developer's laptop does not.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- build output
**/bin/
**/obj/
**/node_modules/
**/dist/

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- version
.git/
.gitignore
.github/

# ------------------------------------------------------------- editor / local
.vs/
.vscode/
.idea/
*.user
*.suo

# --------------------------------------------------------------------- secrets
# .env holds the compose secrets. Copying it into a layer would bake the SA
# password and the JWT signing key into the image, where anyone who can pull it
# can read them back out with `docker history`.
.env
.env.*
!.env.docker.example
**/secrets.json

# ----------------------------------------------------------------- runtime data
# Uploaded attachments belong in a volume, not in the image.
**/app-data/
**/logs/
*.log

# ------------------------------------------------------- not needed by the API
tests/
docs/
tools/
frontend/
*.md
docker-compose.yml
